---
export const title = 'Sorting';
export const order = 250;
import DemoComponentCard from '../../../../components/DemoComponentCard.astro';

import dataSource from '../_data-source.json';
---

<p>
  Clicking a header sorts by that column: ascending, then descending, then unsorted.
  <kbd>Shift</kbd>+click (or <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> on a focused header) adds the column
  as the next sort level instead; a small number on each sorted header shows its priority. A plain
  click starts over with that column alone.
</p>
<p>
  The <code>sort</code> event reports <code>{'{ sortBy, sortOrder, sorts }'}</code>:
  <code>sortBy</code> and <code>sortOrder</code> describe the primary level, <code>sorts</code>
  every level as <code>{'{ name, order }'}</code>. Set the <code>sorts</code> property to sort by
  several columns from code; <code>sort-by</code> and <code>sort-order</code> still set a single
  sort. The primary header carries <code>aria-sort</code>; other levels name their priority to
  screen readers. Turn sorting off by setting the table's <code>sortable</code> property to
  <code>false</code>, or with <code>sortable: false</code> on a column. In <code>managed</code> mode the table only reports the
  sort; sort the data yourself.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang='html'>
  <div style='width: 100%; height: 24rem;'>
    <wc-table id='table-sorting' key-field='_id' label='People'></wc-table>
  </div>

  <script is:inline define:vars={{ dataSource }}>
    const $table = document.getElementById('table-sorting');
    $table.columns = [
      { name: 'eyeColor', label: 'Eye color', width: 9 },
      { name: 'age', label: 'Age', width: 6, type: 'number' },
      { name: 'company', label: 'Company', width: 12, flex: 1 },
    ];
    $table.data = dataSource;
    $table.sorts = [
      { name: 'eyeColor', order: 'asc' },
      { name: 'age', order: 'desc' },
    ];
  </script>
</DemoComponentCard>
